REQ-ZRM-BASE
When the analysis dictionary contains a configuration block derived from ZermeloTrjGen,
the program shall execute a trajectory-generation workflow
for a specified single start point and a single destination point.
Implementation
ZermeloTrjGen shall:
-own common configuration parsing and validation;
-determine the trajectory space from the common height-range settings;
-operate in two modes (midHeight or freeHeight) specified by "variableHeight": if false look for the trajecotry in the midheight, if true look for the 3D trajectory.
-read optional startPoint and endPoint from the top level of
  system/fsStreamLineDict as three-component points in the route plane;
  default them to the length-weighted centers of the start and destination
  patch intersections at the corridor midheight;
  for midHeight mode, validate that start and end point do not significantly differ by height (compare with device size or small value)
-read the obstaclePatches, use ground as the default value for obstaclePatches;
-use the common non-negative minObstacleClearance from fsStreamLineDict, defaulting to zero;
-accept zero clearance as disabling obstacle expansion;
-require a positive deviceSpeed when specified, use 0.1 m/s as the default deviceSpeed;
-compute intersections between the trajectory space and the configured obstacle patches;
-prepare obstacle representations expanded by minObstacleClearance;
-invoke a derived-class trajectory-construction strategy to find the shortest
  feasible trajectory between the specified points;
-compute trajectory length from the trajectory geometry returned by the derived strategy;
-compute travel time from trajectory length and deviceSpeed;
-collect the trajectory result;
-if no valid trajectory exists, do not write trajectory output and report this
  condition in the application log;
If variableHeight is enabled and the selected generator does not support variable-height routing,
the program shall terminate with a configuration error.
Related class: ZermeloTrjGen

REQ-ZRM-OUTPUT
The program shall write Zermelo trajectory results separately from streamline results for the specified start and end points.
Implementation
The results shall be written to the analysis case's postProcessing directory.
Depending on the used solver naming_suffix for output file names will be specified.
File postProcessing/zermelo-(naming_suffix)-tracks.vtk shall contain the trajectory
geometry.
File postProcessing/zermelo-(naming_suffix)-lines.vtk shall contain the trajectory
geometry and, for every optimal trajectory:
-trajectory identifier;
-trajectory length;
-travel time.
If no feasible trajectory exists, no trajectory files shall remain as output;
the application shall report this condition in its log.
Trajectory output shall be produced through a dedicated Zermelo trajectory
writer.
Related class: ZermeloTrajectoryWriter

REQ-ZRM-NOFLOW
When the analysis dictionary contains a ZermeloTrjNoFlow block, the program shall generate a collision-free baseline trajectory between the specified single start point and single destination point without using flow-field information.
The generated trajectory shall be purely geometric and shall not depend on velocity, pressure, turbulence, or any other flow-field quantity. If no feasible trajectory exists, it shall be reported as unreachable and no trajectory geometry shall be generated.
Implementation
ZermeloTrjNoFlow shall:
- derive from ZermeloTrjGen;
- use "no-flow" suffix for marking the data (e.g. in output file names);
- generate the trajectory in a fixed horizontal plane located at the midpoint of minHeight and maxHeight;
- use the single start point, single destination point, and expanded obstacle footprints (offset by minObstacleClearance) prepared by ZermeloTrjGen;
- read optional collapseLength from the ZermeloTrjNoFlow configuration block as the waypoint-collapse distance in metres; require a finite non-negative value and default to 0.5 m, with zero disabling waypoint collapse;
- construct a visibility graph using the vertices of the expanded obstacle footprints and the route endpoints;
- determine the shortest collision-free piecewise-linear trajectory between the specified start point and destination point;
- ensure that no trajectory segment enters the interior of an expanded obstacle; visibility-graph contact with obstacle boundaries is permitted;
- return the trajectory geometry to the base workflow for result processing;
- report the route as unreachable when no collision-free path exists;
- report progress in the application log during patch intersection, obstacle footprint preparation, visibility-graph vertex preparation, and shortest-path search (with search progress showing completed candidate checks relative to the maximum possible visibility-graph candidate checks);
- enforce that variable-height routing is outside the scope of this generator; if variableHeight is enabled, terminate with a configuration error.
Related class
ZermeloTrjNoFlow : public ZermeloTrjGen

REQ-ZRM-FULL
When the analysis dictionary contains a ZermeloTrjFull block, the program shall generate an optimal, wind-corrected trajectory between the specified single start point and single destination point 
by approximating the full Zermelo navigation problem using fluid flow-field information (velocity field U) and preserving waypoints (knots) from ZermeloNoFlow trajectory.
If a valid baseline no-flow trajectory file does not already exist in the postProcessing directory, the program shall compute it only when the analysis dictionary also contains a ZermeloTrjNoFlow block; otherwise, the full route shall be reported unavailable without launching no-flow calculations.
Using the sequence of knots (waypoints) from this baseline trajectory, the full Zermelo problem shall be solved between consecutive baseline knots, preserving the baseline knot sequence and incorporating local flow-aware optimization.
Implementation
ZermeloTrjFull shall:
- derive from ZermeloTrjGen;
- use "passive" suffix for marking the data (e.g. in output file names);
- check the postProcessing directory for an existing baseline no-flow trajectory file;
  if absent, invoke ZermeloTrjNoFlow to generate the geometric visibility graph baseline and its constituent knots (waypoints),
  otherwise extract from the no-flow trajectory file the knots for further computes;
  invoke the no-flow generator only when the analysis dictionary contains an explicit ZermeloTrjNoFlow block;
  after generation, reuse the baseline generator's in-memory knots instead of revalidating its serialized VTK output.
- report baseline waypoint or segment intersections with expanded obstacle interiors as warnings and continue full-route optimization; do not treat these checks alone as a reason to cancel calculations.
- read velocity field $U$ from the latest numeric time step in the analyzed case, if it is absent, show warning and cancel calculations;
- extract the ordered sequence of knots from the baseline path;
- use the ordered sequence of knots from the baseline trajectory as mandatory optimization vertices defining the route topology;
- between each pair of consecutive baseline knots (including start and destination), construct a local flow-aware optimization graph using CFD velocity field UUU, obstacle vertices, and additional flow-sampling points;
- determine the minimum-travel-time connection between each pair of consecutive baseline knots and combine the resulting segment trajectories into a single route;
- the final route shall preserve the baseline knot ordering and shall not skip intermediate baseline knots;
- these segment solutions shall then be combined into a single continuous curve and globally optimized for minimum TTF.
- use the single start point, single destination point, and expanded obstacle footprints (offset by minObstacleClearance) prepared by ZermeloTrjGen;
- ensure that no segment of the wind-corrected path enters the interior of an expanded obstacle;
- report the route as unreachable when no feasible wind-corrected path exists;
- report progress in the application log during baseline verification, segment-by-segment Zermelo solving, and curve optimization;
- enforce configuration error handling if variableHeight configuration constraints are violated.
Related class
ZermeloTrjFull : public ZermeloTrjGen